This is the repository for the VDMC_DMFT code: arXiv.2503.11440
The dataset is uploaded at: https://zenodo.org/records/15086182

for any questions, please contact me at yw975@physics.rutgers.edu

environment requirements:
python
numpy
scipy
matplotlib
mpi4py
numba

structure of the code(when you download all data and source codes they thould look like this):
project_root/
│── src/                      # source code
    ├──DMFT_CT.py             # calculate DMFT counter terms
    ├──pert_DMFT_PM.py        # main code of doing perturbation
    ├──pert_DMFT_PM_energy.py # calculation and plotting of magnetization and total energy, specific heat
    ├──pert_DMFT_PM_B.py      # code of doing perturbation under a small magnetic field.
    ├──XAF.py                 # calculate susceptibility
    ├──DMFT/                  # This folder may contains something useful if you want to run your own DMFT
    ├──diagramsMC/            # some functions about doing Monte Carlo    
    ...(other libraries)
│── data/                     # data
    ├── files_ctqmc/          # DMFT data from CT-QMC impurity solver
    ├── files_boldc/          # DMFT data from bold-CTQMC impurity solver
    ├── files_boldcB/         # DMFT data from bold-CTQMC impurity solver, under a small magnetic field (to calculate the X_AF)
    ├── Sigma_disp1/          # self-energy calculated from perturbation
    ├── Sigma_dispB/          # self-energy calculated from perturbation, under a small magnetic field (to calculate the X_AF)
    ├── Sigma_imp/            # DMFT counter terms in perturbation
│── .gitignore      
│── README.md 



How to run the code:

if you want to start from the scratch, start from step 1;
if you want to use my DMFT data and do the perturbation, start from step 2.
if you want to take all my data and check detailed results, start from step 4. (strongly recommended)


1.This code requires Dynamical Mean Field Theory (DMFT) data. To get DMFT data, you can:
    1. Use my DMFT data uploaded in zenodo: https://zenodo.org/records/15086182. (strongly recommended)
    2. Use the DMFT package from:  http://hauleweb.rutgers.edu/tutorials/index.html and use its CT-QMC impurity solver to generate the DMFT data.
    3. Use your favorite DMFT code (if you have one) to generate the DMFT data.


2. after doing DMFT, First we have to prepare the DMFT counter terms.
run the function gen_allCTs in DMFT_CT.py to get the DMFT counter term it will be saved in the folder 'Sigma_imp'.
Note: DMFT counter terms are alpha-independent. 


3. To do the perturbation at given U, T, and given several alphas, use launch_UT(U,T,maxorder) in src/pert_DMFT_PM.py.
maxorder=3 will be very fast, but maxorder=4 takes much longer time since Monte Carlo sampling is used (which is not very efficient...).
it will read the DMFT counter term and do the perturbation, and save the self-energy through several coefficients.


4. After the perturbation is done, to get the magnetization and total energy for specific U and Tunder different variational parameters and orders:
run mag_test() or energy_test() in src/pert_DMFT_energy.py
it will read stored data and calculate the total energy and magnetization and plot it.


5. To get the total energy/CV curve: run energy_fastconv(8.0,1,1) in if __name__ == "__main__": of src/pert_DMFT_energy.py
Cv is hard to calculate since it's derivative of energy, which will bring a lot of noise.

6. To get the antiferromagnetic susceptibility: 
    1. run search_optalp(U,T,B,3) in if __name__ == "__main__": of src/pert_DMFT_PM_B.py to generate data under magnetic field.
    2.run X_VDMCAF(10.0) in if __name__ == "__main__": of src/XAF.py to calculate the susceptibility.

7. To see the spectra: It's easy to have spectra once you have the self-energies. But you need to use analytical continuation to get the spectra.




